================================================================================
Where the rules come from
================================================================================

  Which files stack and which have one owner. That every enabled pack's copy of a
  font id is loaded and their providers concatenated, rather than the top pack's
  file replacing the others, is the behaviour of the client's font manager; it is
  observable in any pack that adds providers to minecraft:default without
  restating vanilla's own, and in AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont, whose
  font/default.json lists three providers of its own and does not stop normal
  text from rendering. Atlas sources are applied in order by the atlas loader, so
  concatenating them is additive in the same way. Models, textures, blockstates
  and shaders resolve to one file from the highest-priority pack.

  What each mergeable file contains — `providers` in a font, `sources` in an
  atlas, a flat object of event definitions in sounds.json, a flat object of keys
  in a translation file, `overrides` in a pre-1.21.4 model, `entries` in an item
  model definition — was read from the vanilla files published by misode/mcmeta
  (https://github.com/misode/mcmeta, assets branch) and from the pack layout
  documented on minecraft.wiki (https://minecraft.wiki/w/Resource_pack).

  The provider types that can be enumerated from the file, `bitmap` through its
  `chars` grid and `space` through the keys of `advances`, and the three that
  cannot — `reference`, `ttf`, `unihex` — come from the same two sources. That
  U+0000 marks an empty cell in a `chars` row comes from the bitmap provider's
  own validation.

  The legacy override rule that makes a duplicated custom_model_data value fatal
  for one of the two items — the last override whose predicates all hold wins —
  and its modern counterpart — a range_dispatch selects the entry with the
  largest threshold that is less than or equal to the value — are the two
  selection rules stated in the item model definition documentation on
  minecraft.wiki and visible in every vanilla definition that uses them.

  The pack.mcmeta rules — the format-64 boundary between the supported_formats
  era and the min_format/max_format era, both new fields being required
  together, supported_formats being rejected above 64, and pack_format needing
  to be at least 15 in a multi-version pack — come from the pack metadata codec
  in the client, via a publicly available decompilation used as read-only
  evidence. No decompiled code is reproduced here.

  The version-to-format table in PackFormats.kt lists releases only and was
  derived from the version summary published by misode/mcmeta, which mirrors
  Mojang's version manifest. The numbers are facts about the game.

================================================================================
Prior art
================================================================================

None of these projects' code is used.

  misode/mcmeta                 no licence file — treated as read-only reference
                                https://github.com/misode/mcmeta
                                The vanilla assets of every version as plain
                                directories, which is what the merge rules were
                                checked against.

  ComunidadAylas/PackSquash     AGPL-3.0      https://github.com/ComunidadAylas/PackSquash
                                Pack optimisation, done properly. Run it after
                                this, as a separate program.

  unnamed/creative              MIT           https://github.com/unnamed/creative
                                A resource pack object model and serialiser for
                                the JVM, with its own notion of merging packs in
                                memory. The right dependency if you are doing
                                this inside a plugin rather than on the command
                                line.

  AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont    CC-BY-4.0     https://github.com/AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont
                                The pack that demonstrates font providers
                                merging across packs rather than replacing each
                                other. Not shipped or reproduced here.

  Oraxen, Nexo, ItemsAdder      Various, some closed or source-available with
                                restrictions. The packs this tool exists to
                                combine, and the source of the conventions it
                                has to respect: items hung off a shared vanilla
                                item, glyphs allocated in the private use area.
                                No code from any of them is present, and none of
                                their config formats is read or written.

  SalyyS1/packmigrate           Apache-2.0    https://github.com/SalyyS1/packmigrate
                                The same author's converter for legacy overrides.
                                Run it before merging if one of the packs still
                                uses them.

